Method
------
I attacked the S3 implementation at 5d91443c: the 27 workspace typecheck
scripts, the four `tsc --noEmit` fallback apps, the keep and fallback lists,
the CI workflow lines, the `typescript6` parse alias, the root `tsc` bin
link, and the gates.sh errno classifier of commit 4f4fc3c4. Each attack ran
as a live probe or as a seeded mutant against the exact guard logic of
scripts/__tests__/oxc-toolchain-gates.test.ts, with a paired control on the
real tree. The committed cases live in describe('S3 adversarial') in
scripts/__tests__/oxc-toolchain-gates.test.ts (adv 1 to adv 7) and in
describe('S3 adversarial') in scripts/__tests__/vinext-oxc-gates-script.test.ts
(the errno case). Commit cb3d1ba1 holds them.

Attack table
------------
(a) A typecheck script that runs no type check. Case 1 compares each script
to the exact target string, so each briefed mutant fails: TSRS_FAST=1
dropped, --type-check dropped, --type-aware dropped, --max-warnings 0
dropped, the budget raised to 1, --config dropped, the wrong config target
(.oxlintrc.json instead of ../../.oxlintrc.json), the wrong path (src for
an app, . for a package), an npx prefix, and an extra flag. Adv 1 drives the
extracted rule typecheckScriptFault over all 17 mutants and the real tree.
Result: caught. See below for the fallback exception (d, F4).

(b) A config that checks no file. Live probe (adv 5): a temp project with an
empty src folder, the exact package target command, TSRS_FAST=1, and the
root config exits 1 with 'No files found to lint'. A missing path also exits
1. The same command with a probe in src reports TS2322. Result: the gate
fails loudly, so no vacuous pass exists. Pinned as a tripwire.

(c) A probe that passes while tsrs is absent or its native binary is
missing. The Red log (red-s3-ba7635c0.log) proves the absent-tool path:
oxlint prints 'Failed to find type-aware linter executable', so S3 cases 6
and 7 fail on their preconditions and S1 cases 5, 5b, and 6 fail loudly on
their output assertions. A missing native binary cannot produce the TS2322
output that case 6 requires, and a type checker that cannot start makes
oxlint exit non-zero, so case 7 (exit 0) fails too. The first-install break
(evidence section 9, 'Cannot find the optional dependency') was caught the
same way before Green closed. Residual: no committed test uninstalls tsrs
to simulate the state; that would mutate node_modules. The defense is the
precondition plus the TS2322 output assertion, both live in cases 6 and 7.

(d) Keep list and fallback drift. Case 5 compares the declared set to
TYPESCRIPT_KEEP_LIST by exact equality, so a dropped workspace or a stale
entry fails. Adv 2 proves it with seeded drift and proves that a
`typescript6` declaration never counts. Two holes were open at 5d91443c and
are now closed in cb3d1ba1: no test asserted the strategy 16.3 subset rule
(TSC_FALLBACK_LIST inside TYPESCRIPT_KEEP_LIST, plan line 413), and case 1
accepted weakened fallback scripts through an includes match. See F2 and
F4.

(e) The parse alias. Adv 3 proves the alias is load-bearing: the hoisted
`typescript` package at HEAD is 7.0.2 and exposes no createSourceFile, no
comment-range functions, and no ScriptKind. A scan import pointed at
`typescript` therefore crashes on the first API call, so the switch cannot
pass silently. Case 0 already pins the alias declaration, the resolve, the
major 6, and the API surface, so an alias that resolves to major 7 fails
case 0.

(f) The root tsc bin. Green proved the shadow in evidence section 9:
without the `@typescript/native` alias, `npx tsc` prints Version 6.0.3
through the `@typescript/old` bin, and no committed test pinned it. That was
an open hole (F3): a reinstall that drops or reorders the alias silently
flips the four fallback gates to TypeScript 6. Adv 4 closes it: the alias
declaration `npm:typescript@^7.0.2`, a live `npx tsc --version` that prints
`Version 7.`, and a bin link that resolves inside @typescript/native.

(g) Hidden tsc calls in CI. Adv 7 drives the extracted case 4 match over
`npx tsc --noEmit`, `npx --yes tsc`, `npx --no-install tsc -p .`, bare
`tsc --noEmit`, both project flags, and a call behind a `cd` chain: each
matches. A multi-line `run: |` block matches on its second line. The
controls stay clean: `npm run typecheck --workspace=...`, the bare word in
prose, an echo, and a comment line do not match (A7 holds). One hole was
open: the case 4 per-file sentinel reads the same readdir list as the loop,
so a workflow file deleted from the disk shrank both lists and nothing
failed; validate-ci.mjs pins only ci.yml and cloudflare-deploy.yml. Adv 7
now pins the five-file set. See F5.

(h) The four fallback apps. No committed test or gate proved that the
`tsc --noEmit` fallback still catches a real type error in BM2, IM1, and
PreCalc; only the manual Verify loop covered IM3. Adv 6 closes it live:
each app gets `__oxc_probe__.ts` at its root, `npm run typecheck
--workspace=<app>` runs the real script, the output names the probe with
TS2322, the probe is deleted in a finally block, and the file is gone after.
Live check: BM2 2.7 s, and the committed loop holds all four apps.

(i) The gates.sh errno classifier of 4f4fc3c4. The committed case proves
both sides with the sourced functions. First: has_infra_output
(gates.sh:27) greps the whole log, so a normal failing-test line whose
title mentions the errno word is classified infrastructure (a false INFRA).
Second: key() (gates.sh:45) drops every line that holds the word, so
new_keys reports no new key when a new failure's diagnostic mentions the
word and the baseline exit is already non-zero: the gate can print
NEWFAIL=0 while a new failure exists. The word appears in this repository
today only in the titles and seeded strings of
scripts/__tests__/vinext-oxc-gates-script.test.ts:352-362, so the false
INFRA fires if those tests ever fail, and the false green needs the word in
a new failing diagnostic. This is F1, a Blocker routed to Green: the fix
narrows the matcher to disk-full message forms (for example `code ENOSPC`,
`ENOSPC: no space left`, `EDQUOT: disk quota`) or applies the infra label
only to jobs that exited 0, and flips the expectations of the committed
adv i case. The case itself pins the current behavior so the fix has a
failing-first target after the flip.

